---

Artix uses syslog-ng or metalog instead of systemd's journald. These write structured log files to /var/log/.

@text
syslog-ng

The most common syslog daemon on Artix/OpenRC systems.

@code bash
# Install syslog-ng
sudo pacman -S syslog-ng

# Enable at boot (OpenRC)
sudo rc-update add syslog-ng default

# Service management
sudo rc-service syslog-ng start
sudo rc-service syslog-ng stop
sudo rc-service syslog-ng restart
sudo rc-service syslog-ng status

# Main config file
# /etc/syslog-ng/syslog-ng.conf

# Test config syntax
sudo syslog-ng --syntax-only

# Run in foreground for debugging
sudo syslog-ng -F -d

@text
syslog-ng Configuration Basics

@code bash
# /etc/syslog-ng/syslog-ng.conf structure:
#
# source s_sys {
#     system();           # /dev/log, /proc/kmsg
#     internal();         # syslog-ng's own messages
# };
#
# destination d_messages {
#     file("/var/log/messages");
# };
#
# destination d_auth {
#     file("/var/log/auth.log");
# };
#
# destination d_kern {
#     file("/var/log/kern.log");
# };
#
# filter f_auth { facility(auth, authpriv); };
# filter f_kern { facility(kern); };
# filter f_cron { facility(cron); };
#
# log { source(s_sys); filter(f_auth); destination(d_auth); };
# log { source(s_sys); filter(f_kern); destination(d_kern); };
# log { source(s_sys); destination(d_messages); };

@text
metalog

A simpler alternative syslog daemon, sometimes used on Artix.

@code bash
# Install metalog
sudo pacman -S metalog

# Enable at boot
sudo rc-update add metalog default

# Service management
sudo rc-service metalog start
sudo rc-service metalog status

# Config: /etc/metalog.conf
# Logs typically go to /var/log/ with subdirectories per facility

@text
Syslog Facilities and Priorities

@code bash
# Facilities (message sources):
# auth/authpriv — authentication, sudo, login
# cron          — cron daemon
# daemon        — system daemons
# kern          — kernel messages
# mail          — mail system
# user          — user-level messages (default)
# local0-local7 — custom application use
# syslog        — syslog daemon itself

# Priorities (severity, high to low):
# emerg   (0) — system is unusable
# alert   (1) — immediate action needed
# crit    (2) — critical conditions
# err     (3) — error conditions
# warning (4) — warning conditions
# notice  (5) — normal but significant
# info    (6) — informational
# debug   (7) — debug messages

# Send a test message via logger
logger "Test message"
logger -p auth.info "Auth test"
logger -p local0.err "Custom facility error"
logger -t myapp "Tagged message"

@text
Viewing Logs

@code bash
# View recent messages
tail -50 /var/log/messages

# Follow log in real-time
tail -f /var/log/messages

# Follow multiple logs
tail -f /var/log/messages /var/log/auth.log

# Search logs
grep -i "error" /var/log/messages
grep -i "failed" /var/log/auth.log

# View logs from a date range
awk '/May 10/,0' /var/log/messages

# View with timestamps parsed
less /var/log/messages
